v1.1
  the speed control is a basic slider now, not a stepped switch. The stepped
  switch turned out to send the number of the position it sits on - 1 to 7 -
  and to ignore the minimum, maximum and step size the driver publishes, so
  every position arrived as a single digit and the fan stayed at 10 %. The
  stepped switch is no longer offered for the speed, and a value that is not a
  valid percentage is refused with a line in the log rather than rounded into
  something the user did not ask for.

  Verified on Venus OS v3.79 against a MaxxFan Deluxe: fan, speed, direction,
  lid, mode and both buttons all behave, and the fan follows the slider.

v1.0
  first release. Publishes the MaxxFan on the Venus OS D-Bus as
  com.victronenergy.switch, with a card in the switch pane holding fan on/off,
  speed, direction, lid, manual/auto, the thermostat setpoint and two buttons
  for re-sending the state and for making the fan beep. The state is kept in
  the Venus settings and survives restarts.

  The Arduino sketch in arduino/ computes the remote control packet from the
  requested state rather than replaying recorded pulse trains, which is what
  makes the setpoint reachable at all. Its 38 kHz carrier comes from timer 1 in
  hardware, so the serial port keeps working while a packet is transmitted -
  earlier sketches disable interrupts for the whole 150 ms and lose commands
  that arrive in that window.

  Two findings from checking the encoder against 99 signals captured from an
  original remote, both of which differ from what the published ESPHome
  component does:
    - the symbol period is 834 us, not 800 us
    - the setpoint is stored in Fahrenheit and the conversion from the Celsius
      the remote displays truncates towards zero, so trunc(C * 1.8) + 32. A
      rounded conversion is off by one degree on 20 of the 38 setpoints.
  tools/verify-encoder.py re-runs that comparison.
